Toolkit for High Energy Physics Event Generation - ThePEG

This branch uses libtool ABI version 31:0:0. The luminosity interface, Cuts
layout and StandardXComb::fill signature require dependent applications and
libraries to be rebuilt. fill reads both luminosity coordinates from a required
parent XComb, checks that they are finite, and checks the collision invariant
mass against the parent and incoming beam momenta. Its unit test covers both
successful restoration and invalid input.

Event and Collision transformations include detached NLO subprocess particles
exactly once, including shared projected particles. JetRegion rapidity ranges
and JetPairRegion detector hemispheres include the beam rapidity. This corrects
asymmetric fixed-beam configurations such as DIS as well as fluctuating beams;
symmetric fixed-beam selections are unchanged.

LesHouchesReader::scan handles event files that lack some of the processes
declared in their init block, and it matches the processes in the events to
the init block by process number rather than by order. Processes that are not
declared are added with a warning. The reader used to read past the end of its
per-process arrays for such files, which could give NaN cross sections.
All processes of a file now share one maximum weight, so processes whose
weights differ keep their relative weights. For IDWTUP = +/-4 the cross
section of a process is its share of the summed weights, so files with
several processes are no longer normalised to about NPRUP times their cross
section. IDWTUP = +/-2 files are normalised to the cross sections declared
in their init block. For IDWTUP = +/-1, as in the Les Houches accord, the
cross section of a process is the mean weight of its own events, so files
with several processes are scanned too, and their cross section is the sum
of these means. A partial scan (MaxScan) estimates the shares of the
processes from the scanned events, with a warning, and so it does for the
mean weight of IDWTUP = -3 files, and of +3 files whose scanned events
have different weights.
The reader used to take the largest XMAXUP times the mean ratio of each
weight to the XMAXUP of its process, which is right only when all XMAXUP are
equal. Files with one process are still not scanned, and a negative XMAXUP
no longer flips the sign of their events. MadGraphReader sets IDWTUP = 1 for
MadEvent files, whose processes appear in proportion to their cross sections
with equal event weights, so it has them normalised as one sample, as for
IDWTUP = +/-4. It scales their weights by the number of events in the
header, or else by the number scanned, now in every initialization and
without dividing by NPRUP. A MadEvent file with one process needs the number
in its header, and so does a partial scan without a cache file. Files whose
normalisation the init block and the scanned events do not determine are
rejected: IDWTUP = +/-2 or +/-3 files with undeclared processes or without
declared cross sections, +/-1 and +/-2 processes that a partial scan
(MaxScan) missed, +/-2 processes whose weights add up to zero or to the
wrong sign, and +/-3 files without scanned events (MaxScan 0) or whose
declared cross sections or event weights add up to zero. An event of a
process that the init block does not declare and the scan did not find,
which the reader used to give weight zero, stops the run. The new virtual
function processesInProportion() is declared last, so the other virtual
functions keep their places, but classes derived from LesHouchesReader must
still be rebuilt.

The event cache of LesHouchesReader (CacheFileName) now works. cacheFile()
returned a copy of the file handle, so the cache was never opened and the
events were read from the event file, and the cache records could not hold
the optional weights. With a cache file the scan now reads and scans every
event, whatever MaxScan says, and writes those that pass the cuts to the
cache; the fraction of the events kept scales the cross section. The run
generates the events from the cache. The initialization of each run
rewrites the cache, so runs at the same time need different cache files. A
truncated cache file is an error. CFile::read() and CFile::write() count
bytes for gzip files but items for plain ones, and the new CFile::type()
tells the reader which.

DecayHandler also provides an optional laboratory decay cylinder. The built-in
GaussianVertexDistribution can smear the interaction point through
StandardEventHandler:VertexDistribution. Both features default to off and are
unsupported for LesHouchesEventHandler. They share the unreleased ABI 31:0:0
transition: clean-rebuild dependents and recreate saved runs. Event geometry,
flight consistency, clone/persistence and stop/resume regressions are included
in make check. Herwig supplies installed LEP geometry example cards.
The event context accepts rotations as well as boosts. Event persistence
version 2 stores the boost and a signed rotation quaternion, including the
spin-1/2 sign, and reads earlier version-1 boost-only contexts.

To compile and install ThePEG follow these steps:

1: run the configure script. To override the defaults: set eg. the
   following environment variables

	CXX: (default g++) your favorite C++11 compiler. Must be
	     standard compliant. g++ version 4.8 or later will do
	     nicely.

   To overwrite the default install prefix (/usr/local) use the
   --prefix=/custom/install/path argument to configure.

   If the LHAPDF library is installed under /usr/local/lib it will be
   used. If it is installed elsewhere, use
   --with-lhapdf=/Path/to/library to specify where.

   If a standard Sun java compiler is available a java GUI called 'thepeg'
   will be installed. To avoid this use --without-javagui.

   'configure --help' will list other environment variables and options which
   will influence the installation.

2: Do 'make check'.
   this will compile the main ThePEG library and two programs in
   the src directory. These will then be run as follows
                ./setupThePEG SimpleLEP.in
   to setup an event generator which is dumped in SimpleLEP.run
	        ./runThePEG SimpleLEP.run
   to run the event generator. Some output will be written in
	SimpleLEP.out, SimpleLEP.log and SimpleLEP.tex.

3: Install ThePEG with 'make install'. This will put all the shared
   libraries in the lib/ThePEG directory under the install path. The
   default repository file 'ThePEGDefaults.rpo' will also be placed
   there. The executables 'setupThePEG' and 'runThePEG' are installed
   in the bin directory under the install path. In the bin directory
   is also installed a java-based setup program called 'thepeg' which
   enables you to setup and run event generators using a GUI. All
   header files will be installed in the 'include/ThePEG' directory
   under the install path, and some sample setup files and a sample
   makefile is installed in the 'share/ThePEG' directory together with
   an emacs lisp file 'ThePEG.el' with some functions for creating
   skeletons of new ThePEG classes.

Gaussian beam energy spectra are available as ThePEG::GaussianBeamLuminosity
in the standard libThePEG library. The MeanEnergyA/B, RelativeSpreadA/B and
NSigma interfaces specify independent, truncated Gaussian spectra for
head-on electron/positron beams. RelativeSpread means sigma(E)/mean(E) and
defaults to zero. The implementation uses the existing GSL dependency;
its deterministic checks are included in 'make check'. See
Handlers/GaussianBeamLuminosity.h for the kinematic conventions.

Note that ThePEG is a framework for implementing event generator
models, and by itself it does not contain enough physics models to
generate realistic events for any collider. Instead programs such as
Pythia7 or Herwig++ should be installed to allow for proper event
generation. 

There is currently no up-to-date manual avaliable. However, the
general structure is described in "PYTHIA version 7-0.0 - a
proof-of-concept version" Comput. Phys. Commun. 134 (2001) 365. (see
also http://www.thep.lu.se/Pythia7/Doc/Proof/). This describes the
Pythia7 program before it was broken up into the basic event generator
platform - ThePEG - and the Pythia-specific parts in Pythia7. 

A brief description of ThePEG is also included in "Herwig++ Physics and Manual"
[arXiv:0803.0883]. In addition, there is Doxygen-generated documentation of the
classes in http://projects.hepforge.org/thepeg/doxygen
The doxygen documentation can also be generated locally by 'make html', in
which it will end up in the Doc/refman-html directory. A subsequent
'make install' will place the documentation in
share/ThePEG/Doc/refman-html.

Briefly, the running of ThePEG is divided into two steps:

1: The program 'setupThePEG' is used to set up an event generator and
   saving it to a file. Here you collect together the components
   needed for a specific event generation run, and parameters and
   switches of the components can be set to preferred values. See the
   file SimpleLEP.in in the src subdirectory for an example.

2: The program 'runThePEG' is used to run the generator saved to a
   file. 

